	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84951940376&partnerID=40&md5=cefae52cd30fa0f4739e401132cf9a01},
	affiliations = {Southern New Hampshire University, Institute of International Business, Department of International Business, Manchester, NH, United States},
	abstract = {The primary focus of this chapter is to measure the influence of higher education in rapidly developing economies in terms of providing for an efficient labor market. The challenges of globalization are explored as well as its profound effect on education policy. The profiled countries in this chapter include: Argentina, Brazil, Chile, China, Egypt, Hungary, India, Indonesia, Malaysia, Mexico, Russia, South Africa, Thailand, Turkey, and United Arab Emirates. These rapidly developing economies (RDEs) are characterized as having high rates of growth and industrialization. The most competitive economies strive to achieve unprecedented advances with regard to innovation and technology to become key players in today's global marketplace without borders. These advances would not be possible without a highly skilled, educated workforce acquiring the necessary 21st century skills and abilities allowing for labor market efficiency. The Boston Consulting Group has identified at least five emerging trends that will shape commerce, not just for the global challengers but for all companies that play on the world stage and vie for the most educated workers. These are: (1) the emergence of Chinese contractors, (2) the rush for natural resources, (3) the rise of diversified global conglomerates, (4) the challenges of building global consumer brands, and (5) the increasing reliance on partnerships. How will these leading existing global economies be able to sustain such growth and efficient labor markets? The answer lies in developing educational policies that will transform the way students acquire a quality education that enables students to develop state of the art skills and talents to meet the demands of a competitive global marketplace. © 2014 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.},
