	abstract = {The innovative approach of batiking on wooden masks, bowls, or boxes has matured into the production of highly refined, beautifully functional handcrafts fully able to hold their own in the contemporary craft marketplace since the early 1990s in tiny villages surrounding Yogyakarta, Java, Indonesia. An event in the 1970s on the sidelines of the traditional Indonesian batik world laid the foundation for the development of this new craft form. Silkscreened cloth was being printed to look like batik and was sold by the meter. It had made deep economic inroads by the early 1990s into the traditional batik industry. Entrepreneur Rudy Supriyadi started to batik on different surfaces like ceramics and leather. Other villages in those dry hills became known for special crafts like woodcarving. Supriyadi's initial idea to push batik into a new direction has spread to many smaller family production operations all over the greater Yogya region.},
